## How Expensive Is Child Care, and Why Subsidies Leave Gaps

Child Care Aware of America, a national nonprofit, reports the average price of child care in 2025 was $13,184 a year. That's up 23% from 2021. Full-time infant care in a center averaged $27,067 in Massachusetts and $6,492 in Mississippi. In all 47 states with data, center care for an infant plus a 4-year-old cost more than median rent.

The main federal subsidy is the Child Care and Development Fund, usually called CCDF. States run it, and many can't serve everyone who qualifies. Indiana is one documented example. It froze new vouchers for about 15 months after December 2024. As of February, roughly 43,000 children were enrolled out of about 300,000 eligible, around 19%.

The federal side is shaky too. In January 2026, the Department of Health and Human Services froze CCDF and related funds for five states: California, Colorado, Illinois, Minnesota and New York. A federal judge temporarily blocked that freeze within days. The situation keeps changing, so treat any subsidy as "check locally," not guaranteed.

My take: don't put all your hope in one program. Apply to several at once, and ask each one about waitlists.

## 1. Head Start and Early Head Start

**What it is:** A federal program that provides free early education, health screenings, meals and family support for children from low-income families. Head Start serves ages 3 to 5. Early Head Start serves babies and toddlers under 3, plus pregnant women. It began in 1965 as part of the War on Poverty, and the program says it has served nearly 40 million children since.

**What it's funded at:** About $12.36 billion for the current federal budget year.

**Who qualifies:** Generally, families at or below the federal poverty line. Under the 2026 guidelines, that's $21,640 for a household of two, $27,320 for three and $33,000 for four. Families can qualify regardless of income if they're homeless, have a child in foster care, or receive TANF or SSI. Programs also reserve some slots for children with disabilities.

**What it costs you:** Nothing.

**How to apply:** Find a local program through the Office of Head Start at [https://www.acf.gov/ohs](https://www.acf.gov/ohs), or call your local Child Care Aware agency (see section 8). Each program runs its own enrollment, so you apply directly to a local grantee.

**Heads-up:**

- Slots are limited, and many programs keep waitlists. Apply early, even if you're not sure you'll need it.
- Programs vary in hours. Some run full days and year-round, and some are part-day.
- In 2025, HHS tried to require immigration status checks for Head Start. A federal court blocked that in September 2025 with a preliminary injunction. That ruling could change, so ask your local program what documents it requires today.

## 2. Free Public Pre-K

**What it is:** State-funded preschool, usually through public schools and partner centers. According to the National Institute for Early Education Research, 44 states and the District of Columbia fund preschool. In its latest report, about 37% of 4-year-olds and roughly 10% of 3-year-olds were enrolled in state programs. States spent about $14.4 billion.

**"Universal" doesn't always mean full-time or guaranteed.** Colorado's universal program covers at least 15 free hours a week. Vermont's has been reported at about 10 hours a week. California's transitional kindergarten is open to every 4-year-old. New York is expanding free pre-K, with universal access for 4-year-olds planned by fall 2028. Many other states limit pre-K by income or need.

**New York City example:** NYC offers free 3-K and pre-K for children turning 3 or 4, and this fall it launched its first 2,000 free 2-K seats. Applications run through [https://myschools.nyc](https://myschools.nyc) or 718-935-2009. The 2026 application windows have closed, but the pattern (apply in winter or spring for the fall) is common elsewhere.

**How to apply:** Search "[your state] free pre-K" and call your school district's early childhood office. Ask about age cutoffs, hours, lotteries and application deadlines.

**Heads-up:** Seats aren't always guaranteed, some programs run lotteries, and hours often don't match a full workday. You may still need before- and after-care.

## 3. New Mexico: Free Child Care for Every Family

**What it is:** On November 1, 2025, New Mexico became the first US state to offer free child care to all families, regardless of income. Families receive state-funded vouchers for public and private child care, with no co-pays. The governor said the average family saves about $12,000 per child a year.

**What changed this year:** The governor signed the program into law on March 10, 2026. The law lets the state draw up to $700 million from its Early Childhood Education and Care Trust Fund over five years. The governor said more than 16,000 new children enrolled in the months after launch.

**How to apply:** Contact the New Mexico Early Childhood Education and Care Department at [https://www.nmececd.org](https://www.nmececd.org).

**Heads-up:**

- It's for New Mexico residents only.
- The state also faces a child care worker shortage, which can limit available slots.
- Funding comes from state revenue and a trust fund, so long-term costs are still being debated.

If you live elsewhere, don't assume your state matches. This is a rare exception, not a national program.

## 4. Tax Help: The Child and Dependent Care Credit and Dependent Care FSA

These don't reduce the bill upfront, but they put real money back.

**The credit:** For 2026, the Child and Dependent Care Credit is worth 20% to 50% of up to $3,000 in care costs for one child, or $6,000 for two or more. That means up to $1,500 for one child and $3,000 for two or more. Lower incomes get the higher rate. You claim it on IRS Form 2441, and details are at [https://www.irs.gov](https://www.irs.gov). Summer day camp can count if the child is under 13 and the care lets you work.

**The Dependent Care FSA:** If your employer offers one, you can set aside up to $7,500 pre-tax for 2026. Your employer has to adopt the higher limit, and you generally elect it during open enrollment. You can't use the same expenses for both the FSA and the credit.

**The Child Tax Credit:** This is separate. It's worth up to $2,200 per qualifying child and doesn't depend on child care costs.

**Heads-up:**

- The care credit is nonrefundable. If you owe little or no tax, you may not get the full amount.
- You'll need your provider's name, address and tax ID or Social Security number.
- Paying a relative can still count, but not if the relative is your tax dependent or your own child under 19.

A tax preparer can tell you whether the FSA or the credit works better for your income.

## 5. Paying a Relative, Friend or Neighbor (Family, Friend and Neighbor Care)

This is the least talked-about option. Many states let subsidy dollars pay a grandparent, aunt, uncle, adult sibling, friend or neighbor, even without a child care license. It's called family, friend and neighbor care, or license-exempt care.

**Examples:**

- **Washington:** lets its subsidy program pay certain relatives and in-home providers. They must be approved, and there's an annual visit.
- **Michigan:** pays license-exempt providers through the parent. Providers must be 18, complete a background check and some training.
- **California:** pays license-exempt providers at 70% of the county's family child care home rate. Non-relatives need a TrustLine background check.

**How to start:** Apply for your state's child care subsidy, then tell the agency you want to use a relative or neighbor as your provider. Rules differ by state.

**Heads-up:**

- You usually need to qualify for the subsidy first, and subsidies can have waitlists.
- The caregiver must pass background checks and may need training.
- Payments may count as taxable income for the caregiver.

## 6. YMCA Financial Assistance

**What it is:** The YMCA was founded in London in 1844 by George Williams. The first US YMCA opened in Boston on December 29, 1851, led by Thomas Valentine Sullivan. Today, many local Ys run child care, preschool, before- and after-school programs and summer camps, with income-based financial aid.

**What you can expect:** Many Ys offer sliding-scale fees and scholarships funded by donations. Some locations accept state child care vouchers. One example: the Honolulu YMCA offers aid to households earning under $65,000, and up to $110,000 for a family of four. Your local Y sets its own limits.

**How to apply:** Find your local branch at [https://www.ymca.org](https://www.ymca.org) and ask for the financial assistance application for child care.

**Heads-up:**

- Aid is limited and often first come, first served.
- You may need to renew every year.
- Some Ys ask you to use your state subsidy first if you qualify.
- Apply well before the program starts. One Y asks for at least a week.

## 7. Campus Child Care for Student Parents

**What it is:** The federal Child Care Access Means Parents in School program, called CCAMPIS, gives grants to colleges to support campus child care for low-income student parents. Many campus programs use sliding fees.

**Where it stands:** The federal government ran a 2026 grant competition this spring, with applications due May 29. Advocates report the next budget proposal would eliminate it, but that isn't law yet.

**How to find help:** Ask your college's student parent office or campus child care center. Details on the program are at [https://www.ed.gov](https://www.ed.gov). If you're going back to school, my post on [FAFSA and Scholarships for Single Parents: Deadlines & How to Apply (Updated Guide)](/blog/single-parent-scholarships) covers the aid side.

**Heads-up:** Not every college has campus child care, waitlists are common, and funding could change.

## 8. Free Local Referral Help and Military Options

**Child Care Aware of America:** This national nonprofit was founded in 1987 as the National Association of Child Care Resource and Referral Agencies. It works with hundreds of local referral agencies. Call 1-800-424-2246 or search your ZIP code at [https://www.childcareaware.org/resources/ccrr-search](https://www.childcareaware.org/resources/ccrr-search).

**What a local referral agency can do:** Give you a list of licensed providers, explain your state's subsidy rules, tell you about waitlists and point you to local programs. In an older report, the network said local agencies helped over 860,000 families a year.

**Other places to start:** The federal portal [https://www.childcare.gov](https://www.childcare.gov) and the 211 helpline at [https://www.211.org](https://www.211.org), or just dial 2-1-1.

**Military families:** Child Care Aware of America runs fee assistance programs for some military families, such as the Air Force's program for civilian child care when on-base care isn't an option. Call 1-800-424-2246 to ask.

**Tip:** Ask whether you fall into a priority group. Some states move families facing homelessness, foster care or other circumstances ahead of the waitlist.

