## Things That Look Available but Aren't

**Free government child care grants for individuals:** These don't exist. Grants.gov says the federal government doesn't contact people to award grants they never applied for. It also says the government doesn't charge fees to apply and doesn't award grants by drawing or raffle.

**A nationwide universal child care program:** There isn't one. New Mexico is the only state I found with free child care for every family.

**A guaranteed Head Start or subsidy slot:** Eligibility isn't the same as getting a seat.

## The Scam Rule: Never Pay for "Free" Child Care Money

Scammers target stressed parents. They may message you on social media, claim you've been awarded a federal child care grant and ask for a fee, gift cards or your bank account number. The FTC has warned about this exact script, including fake offers that claim to come from HHS.

**Red flags:**

- You're told you won a grant you never applied for.
- They ask for a "processing fee," a deposit or gift cards.
- They ask for your bank login or full Social Security number up front.
- The message came through social media, text or a surprise phone call.
- The sender isn't on a .gov website.

Real applications go through official sites and never require a fee. If you're targeted, report it at [https://reportfraud.ftc.gov](https://reportfraud.ftc.gov). You can learn how real federal grants work at [https://www.grants.gov](https://www.grants.gov), though those grants go to organizations, not individuals.

## Your 30-Day Child Care Action Plan

**Week one:** Call Child Care Aware at 1-800-424-2246 or dial 2-1-1. Ask about subsidies, waitlists, Head Start and free pre-K near you.

**Week two:** Apply to Head Start, your state subsidy and any pre-K program, all at once. Put your name on every waitlist.

**Week three:** Ask your local YMCA and your college (if you're a student) about financial aid. Gather documents: ID, proof of income, your child's birth certificate and your work or school schedule.

**Week four:** Line up a backup, like a trusted relative who could be approved for payment. Check whether your employer offers a Dependent Care FSA, and mark open enrollment on your calendar. Start a folder for care receipts and provider tax IDs.

## Free Child Care Help FAQs

**Is there really free child care in the US?**

Yes, but it depends on where you live and who you are. Head Start is free for eligible families, many states offer free pre-K, and New Mexico offers it to everyone.

**What if I'm on a waitlist?**

Stay on it and apply to other programs at the same time. Call monthly, and ask whether you qualify for a priority group.

**Can I pay my mom or a neighbor to watch my child?**

In many states, a subsidy can pay a relative or neighbor if they meet the rules. You may also be able to count the cost toward the tax credit, depending on who they are.

**I don't qualify for a subsidy. What are my options?**

Look at the tax credit, a Dependent Care FSA, YMCA sliding scales, free pre-K for your child's age and referral help from your local agency.

## The Bottom Line

No single program solves child care, and anyone promising one is selling something. Real help comes from stacking a few pieces: a free program where you qualify, a tax benefit and a backup plan. Start now, because waitlists move slowly.

